From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:50:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] usb: ohci-platform: Power the root hub PHYs up before
 resuming the controller

The USB core powers the root hub PHYs off when it suspends the root hub
for system sleep (usb_phy_roothub_suspend() in hcd_bus_suspend()), and
only powers them up again in hcd_bus_resume(). For a platform OHCI the
controller's own system resume callback runs before that, so
ohci_platform_resume() calls ohci_resume() - which reads HcControl and,
if the controller lost power, restarts it - while the PHYs are still
down.

On RK3588 the OHCI registers are not accessible in that state: once the
USB power domain (PD_USB, which genpd switches off in suspend_noirq) has
been power-cycled, the first OHCI register access from
ohci_platform_resume() hangs the bus. On the Orange Pi 5 every s2idle
resume then wedges the SoC: an xHCI on the same SoC stops completing
commands ("Error while assigning device slot ID: Command Aborted"), CPUs
stop answering IPIs and even pseudo-NMIs, and only the watchdog brings
the board back. A single HcControl read is enough; the EHCI companion,
which shares the PHY, can access its registers in the same state.

Bring the root hub PHYs up around ohci_resume(). The PHY framework
counts the calls, so this nests with the companion controller and with
the root hub's own resume: if the PHY is already up nothing happens, and
the PHYs end up in the same state hcd_bus_resume() expects. The suspend
path is not changed: there the PHYs are already down too, but the
controller has not lost power yet and its registers are still accessible.

Tested on Orange Pi 5 (RK3588S): fast s2idle (no slow serial console,
which happened to hide the problem) wedged on every attempt before,
with the PHYs powered around an unmodified ohci_resume() it resumes
cleanly.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/usb/host/ohci-platform.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/host/ohci-platform.c b/drivers/usb/host/ohci-platform.c
index c801527d5bd2..1d16229a81fd 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/host/ohci-platform.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/ohci-platform.c
@@ -31,6 +31,7 @@
 #include <linux/usb/of.h>
 
 #include "ohci.h"
+#include "../core/phy.h"
 
 #define DRIVER_DESC "OHCI generic platform driver"
 #define OHCI_MAX_CLKS 4
@@ -312,8 +313,27 @@ static int ohci_platform_resume_common(struct device *dev, bool hibernated)
 		}
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The USB core powered the root hub PHYs off when it suspended the
+	 * root hub, and only powers them up again in hcd_bus_resume(), which
+	 * runs after this. On some SoCs (RK3588) the OHCI registers are not
+	 * accessible while the PHY is down: ohci_resume() would hang the bus.
+	 * Keep the PHYs up while ohci_resume() touches the controller. The
+	 * PHY framework counts these calls, so this nests with the companion
+	 * controller and with the root hub's own resume.
+	 */
+	err = usb_phy_roothub_resume(hcd->self.sysdev, hcd->phy_roothub);
+	if (err) {
+		if (pdata->power_off)
+			pdata->power_off(pdev);
+		reset_control_assert(priv->resets);
+		return err;
+	}
+
 	ohci_resume(hcd, hibernated);
 
+	usb_phy_roothub_suspend(hcd->self.sysdev, hcd->phy_roothub);
+
 	pm_runtime_disable(dev);
 	pm_runtime_set_active(dev);
 	pm_runtime_enable(dev);
